  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential for heavy rain and flooding Sunday-\nTuesday.\n\nStrengthening south-southwest flow in advance of a slow moving\nupper trough in the Ohio Valley will drive anomalous 1.5-2\" PW \ninto CPA along and ahead of an approaching cold front today. A\nleading batch of rain moved from Chambersburg northeast through\nWilliamsport early this morning, dropping a quick half an inch\nto inch of rain. Strong warm advection and moisture \ntransport with the nose of a strong 850mb jet combined with \nfavorable upper dynamics in the form of a strong - albeit\nweakening - trough should support multiple rounds of showers \nand storms today and tonight.\n\nMore widespread showers will overspread from southwest to\nnortheast this morning and continue into the afternoon. Rainfall\nrates of 1 to 2 inches per hour appear likely with the strongest\nstorms. Several convection allowing models depict a lull in the\nprecipitation this afternoon ahead of the cold front followed by\nanother slug of showers/storms with the front itself. Storms\nwill be moving quickly today in the presence of 20-30kt steering\nwinds, but the orientation of wind vectors is basically parallel\nto the approaching cold front. This will encourage\nrepeat/training showers and storms oriented southwest to\nnortheast. Hi- res guidance and ensembles depict potential for \ncorridors of 3\"+ rainfall from the Great Lakes into Central PA.\nThis area continues to be in a Slight Risk for flash flooding\nfrom WPC. In collaboration with the neighbors, we issued a Flood\nWatch from 10AM this morning to 2AM tonight for basically along\nand west of I-99 where the guidance has been most consistent\nwith the highest rainfall totals. Huntingdon County is not\ncurrently included, but may need to be added if river flooding\nalong the Juniata becomes more likely.\n\nAlthough flash flooding is the primary threat today, isolated\nsevere weather is also possible. There remains model spread on \nthe degree of instability built up by this evening, which will \nlikely depend on how much sunshine is able to break through the \nclouds behind the morning precipitation. So uncertainty remains \nregarding where the best cores of convection will end up and the\ncoverage they will occupy. Should there be cloud breaks that \nraise CAPE values, adequate shear and lapse rates may allow for \nisolated damaging wind gusts in Western/Central PA. There is \nalso sufficient spin the lowest levels such that SPC added a 2% \ntornado area across western PA. Of course, any stronger storms\nwill also produce heavier rainfall and locally enhance the risk\nfor flash flooding as well. Showers and storms should lift\neast/dissipate after midnight Sunday night.\n\nHeading into the work week, the upper level trough will close \noff and slows its forward progress over the Ohio River Valley.  \nSynoptic ascent will remain in place over Pennsylvania and \nallow for daily showers/storms and an attendant risk for\nisolated flooding. Marginal risks of flooding are in place for\nat least a portion of Central PA every day through Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Outlook for first half of August is warm and wet\n\nMedium and long range numerical weather prediction guidance\nsignals a prolonged stretch of rainy and warm (but not hot)\nweather across Pennsylvania for the first two weeks of August.\nHigh temperatures will peak late this week and weekend in the \n80s with an outside chance at some 90F readings in the lower \nSusquehanna Valley. The building heat and humidity will also\ngenerate daily afternoon showers and storms. \n\nTaking a broad view at the upcoming weather pattern, ensemble \naccumulated rainfall plots show a steady increase in rainfall \namounts and a notable absence of prolonged dry spells. In other\nwords, most days will see at least some precipitation across the\narea. This will be a significant difference from August of \n2025, which saw hot and extremely dry conditions to start \nAugust. In fact, many locations saw zero rainfall in the first \ntwo weeks of August last year. \n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\nJuly = record-setting month in terms of convective warnings. \nWFO CTP issued 12 Tornado Warnings in July, which is the most \nsince 1996 when we issued 25. 95 Severe Thunderstorm Warnings is\nthe 3rd most in any July on record, behind 1996 (96) and 2021 \n(122). Year-to- date, 226 Severe Thunderstorm Warnings is the \nmost on record, passing 2019 (221), and 24 Tornado Warnings is \n4th most behind 1998 (55), 2011 (37), and 1996 (30). 250 total \nSevere Thunderstorm Warnings + Tornado Warnings is the most on \nrecord year-to-date, passing 2019 (241).",
